{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And she that is sick with her menstrual impurity.</b> The ordering of the above pericopes is as follows: first it speaks of the pericope of the זב (a man with a flux) — ״איש איש כי יהיה זב מבשרו״ — and afterward ״ואשה אשר ישכב איש אותה״, for the verse above speaks of one who has a seminal emission (בעל קרי); therefore here it warns: ״זאת תורת הזב ואשר תצא ממנו שכבת זרע לטמאה בה״. Afterward it mentions the pericope of ״ואשה כי תהיה זבה״, and concerning her it mentions here ״והדוה בנדתה״. Afterward it says at the end of the pericope: ״ואם שכב ישכב איש אותה ותהי נדתה עליו״, and afterward it mentions the pericope of ״ואשה כי יזוב זוב דמה ימים רבים״, and concerning them it says here ״והזב את זובו לזכר ולנקבה ולאיש אשר ישכב עם טמאה״." "<b>And according to</b> the order of the pericopes, it would have been proper to put first and say <b>ולאיש אשר ישכב עם טמאה</b> and to conclude the pericope with <b>לזכר ולנקבה</b>, saying thus: <b>והדוה בנדתה והאיש אשר ישכב עם טמאה והזב את זובו לזכר ולנקבה</b>, for thus is the ordering of the pericopes if you examine them. But in my opinion He changed the matter so that the conclusion of the pericope would be with the word טמאה, in order to juxtapose it with the death of the sons of Aharon, and to hint through this that their death was because they entered the Sanctuary in impurity, for this is one of the opinions that the Rabbis, of blessed memory, gave in their Midrash concerning the sin of Nadav and Avihu: <b>some say</b> on account of strange fire, and so it is explicit in the verse (Leviticus 10): ״ויקריבו לפני ה' אש זרה״, and it is written immediately after it: ״ותצא אש מלפני ה'״. <b>Some say</b> they were intoxicated with wine, which is derived from the juxtaposition, for the verse juxtaposes their sin to ״יין ושכר אל תשת״. <b>And some say</b> they ruled a halakhah in the presence of their teacher, derived from the fact that the verse juxtaposes to it ״ולהורות״. <b>And some say</b> because they remained without wives, for they did not wish to marry, and many women were left waiting for them (עגונות), and they were arrogant, saying: our father's brother is king, our father is High Priest, our mother's brother is prince of Israel, we are officers of the priesthood — which woman is worthy of us? Therefore they were punished, as it is said (Psalms 78): ״בחוריו אכלה אש ובתולותיו לא הוללו״ — why did the fire consume his young men? Because his maidens were not given in marriage. <b>And some say</b> because of the approach to Mount Sinai, that they gazed more than was fitting, and on account of this they died; and the Holy One, blessed be He, waited for them until now so as not to mingle mourning with the rejoicing over the Torah, and this is what is written: ״אחרי מות שני בני אהרן בקרבתם לפני ה'״ — that is, on account of the approach to Mount Sinai, for there Moshe had already warned them, saying (Exodus 19): ״והכהנים והעם״ etc. <b>And some say</b> because they entered the Sanctuary in impurity — that is, without sanctifying their hands and feet, which is punishable by death, as it is said (Exodus 30): ״ורחצו ידיהם ורגליהם ולא ימותו״. Therefore the pericope concludes with ״ולאיש אשר ישכב עם טמאה״, and juxtaposed to it is ״אחרי מות״. Here, then, are six opinions concerning their sin, and it is possible that they sinned in all of them.<br><b>And perhaps</b> what is written ״ותאכל אותם״ spelled full with a ״ו״ alludes to this. Besides that it contains an allusion to what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, mentioned at the end of the first chapter of Yoma: that there are six fires, and I have already mentioned them there." "<b>And in the Midrash,</b> ״והדוה בנדתה״ — the verse likened the impurity of Israel to the impurity of the menstruant (נדה), for the menstruant becomes impure and becomes pure, as it is said (Ezekiel 36): ״כטומאת הנדה היתה דרכם לפני״ — it did not say ״like the impurity of the dead״ but ״like the impurity of the menstruant״, for a corpse, while it is in the house, the priest does not enter there, for he would become impure; but He likened her to the menstruant, for indeed the menstruant — the priest enters with her and sits on the bench (אצטבא). Thus, had He likened Israel to the impurity of the dead, the Shekhinah would never return upon them; rather, He likened them to the menstruant, with whom the priest enters into the house without concern. So too the Holy One, blessed be He, causes His Shekhinah to dwell upon Israel even though they are impure among the nations of the world, as it is said (Leviticus 16): ״השוכן אתם בתוך טמאותם״. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: in this world you are impure, but in the time to come I shall purify you with an everlasting purification, as it is said (Ezekiel 36): ״וזרקתי עליכם מים טהורים וטהרתם מכל טמאותיכם ומכל גלוליכם אטהר אתכם״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:25:58.525084898Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}